<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Evidence ladder</p>
    <h2>We use different evidence for different questions</h2>
    <p><strong>Established physiology</strong> helps explain processes such as sodium-glucose cotransport and fluid balance. <strong>Formulation evidence</strong> explains why ingredients and concentrations are selected. <strong>Product-specific research</strong> evaluates the finished formulation under defined conditions. <strong>Application evidence</strong> asks whether those findings translate to performance, clinical or humanitarian settings.</p>
    <p>Moving up that ladder requires new data. A plausible mechanism does not automatically prove a meaningful product effect, and a result in one population does not automatically apply to another.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Academic collaboration</p>
    <h2>Research is stronger when roles and affiliations are clear</h2>
    <p>Dr. Spencer Proctor is a Professor and Department Chair in Agricultural, Food and Nutritional Science at the University of Alberta and Director of the Metabolic and Cardiovascular Diseases Laboratory. His University of Alberta profile describes a research program spanning nutrition, metabolism, physiology, behaviour, food health and chronic disease.</p>
    <p>Best Hydrate's research pages distinguish academic affiliations from company roles. Collaboration with a university researcher does not mean that the University of Alberta endorses every Best Hydrate product claim, nor does it convert company-led work into an independent clinical trial.</p>
    <p>That distinction matters because credibility depends as much on transparent study ownership and methods as it does on recognizable institutions.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Research convention</span>
    <h3>Affiliation, authorship, funding and study status should be visible.</h3>
    <p>As projects mature, we will identify who led the work, what stage it reached and where results can be independently checked.</p>
  </aside>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">From question to impact</p>
  <h2>A responsible translation pathway</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__steps">
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Define the hydration question</h3><p>Start with a specific population, context, mechanism or practical problem rather than a broad marketing claim.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Choose the right measurement</h3><p>Use outcomes that fit the question, such as fluid balance, urine measures, gastrointestinal tolerance, performance, adherence or clinically relevant endpoints.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Report what actually happened</h3><p>Distinguish planned methods, completed data and statistical findings. Negative, null and mixed results remain informative.</p></article>
    <article class="bhd-page__step"><h3>Translate only as far as the evidence allows</h3><p>Update product education, research priorities and application language without extending a result beyond the studied formulation, dose, setting or population.</p></article>
  </div>
</section>

